\] Next, we apply $r$ to $-\frac{1}{29}$: \[
r\left(-\frac{1}{29}\right) = \frac{1}{1 - \left(-\frac{1}{29}\right)} = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{1}{29}} = \frac{1}{\frac{30}{29}} = \frac{29}{30}
\] Next, we apply $r$ to $\frac{29}{30}$: \[
r\left(\frac{29}{30}\right) = \frac{1}{1 - \frac{29}{30}} = \frac{1}{\frac{1}{30}} = 30
\] We see that applying $r$ four times returns us to the original value. Therefore, applying $r$ six times will be the same as applying $r$ twice since six is two cycles of four plus two. \[
r(r(30)) = r\left(-\frac{1}{29}\right) = \frac{29}{30}
\] So, $r(r(r(r(r(r(30)))))) = \frac{29}{30}$. </think> <answer> $\frac{29}{30}$ </answer>

